The problem is a variation of the Longest Common Prefix (LCP) problem. We can use dynamic programming to solve it. We create an array dp where dp[i] is the length of the longest substring of s that ends at index i and is present in the dictionary.

We initialize dp[i] to i, which means the longest substring that ends at index i is the substring itself.

Then, for each substring s[j:i], we check if it is present in the dictionary. If it is, we update dp[i] to be the minimum of its current value and dp[j]. This is because we can break the substring s[j:i] into two parts: the part from j to k (which is already in the dp array) and the part from k to i (which is the new substring).

